Z||000000||Voyd||12-17-1999||10:38 AM||||im real new to Fieros, got mine only 2 months ago. i only know the basic stuff as far as mechanics go, but i know even less about the Fieros internals, with the engine in the back and stuff. what id like to know is getting a parts car a good idea for the basis of learning on (and using its parts when the need arises of course)? im kinda afraid to fiddle inside my Fiero for fear of screwing sumthin up. its my only car and i depend on it every day for transportation.<P>whats a good price for a parts car generally? i know of a 86 SE which has a good engine and runs well, but has been in a pretty big crash and the whole front is basically torn off. still has good side body panels, a nice spolier, groundeffects, etc. <P>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||dogtired||12-17-1999||11:05 AM||||Sounds like you may have found a great parts car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>And that is a great way to learn, you don't have to disamble your driver to see how it works.<BR>Sometimes the subject of parts cars is volatile due to the diminishing quantities of available cars to restore. Some feel you can restore anything, (which is true) if you have enough resources (money). My thoughts here are why spend 6 8 10 thousand to restore a car when you can purchase one in relative good shape for 1/2 that? I personally don't like to see any car go to the crusher but there comes a time when dollars and parking space runs out.<BR>I would say GO FOR IT! it is usually the cheepest way to get parts. It along with a good manual, you will be helping the rest of us on HOW TO subjects soon. GOOD LUCK  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-17-1999||02:39 PM||||I think that the only trashed Fieros worth restoring (irregardless of the cost) are the "rare" ones like the Indy.  Be aware that there are a few "rare" options you might not want to tear apart such as the lumbar seats.  That's the only one that comes to mind, but there are a few others.  If you can't get something apart and the manual doesn't explain it to your satisfaction, be sure to ask here before you just rip something off.  There are hidden fasteners in abundance on these.  (i.e. the interior sail panels, etc.)  Just food for thought.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voyd||12-17-1999||03:32 PM||||im sure everyone here would like to restore a Fiero from top to bottom and some on here probably did it already, but for the vast majority its just way too much money. i agree that it kind of sucks that by having a parts car you are reducing the amount of Fieros in the world by 1...but if not for parts cars we wouldnt be able to have all our cars still look so darn good after all these years  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:55 PM||||A few must be sacrificed to save many... this is a recurring theme in life. <P>WOW, I'm deep!  LOL<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||12-17-1999||08:23 PM||||A badly wrecked Fiero makes a good parts car. There's probably too much frame damage to restore it. I'd probably not give much more than a hundred bucks for one though.||||reg||1
